  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$  in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  According to the diagram, we have $f(4)=1$ (we have an arrow from $4\\in X$ to $1\\in Y$). So, there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=4.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $4\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
